t(\\int u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679475} :answer-field/id #uuid "74724f24-3864-5dbf-9f26-2a473f015706" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679472}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f6584c0-0c89-55ec-8ed8-15570be602ff" :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=e^{2 x} + 4$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int \\left(e^{2 x} + 4\\right)^{3} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Let $u=e^{2 x} + 4$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 4>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\left(e^{2 x} + 4\\right)^{3} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186679464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fbb508d-92db-5035-8a17-fd24237b638d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(\\ln\\left|- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1\\right|\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fbb508d-92db-5035-8a17-fd24237b638d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(\\ln\\left|- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1\\right|\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679488} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc081b0-2f19-5297-9456-fed742bd694e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679469}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65cd622a-f56e-5266-8bca-f7152ab8c719" :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4 e^{2 x} - 4}{- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\ln\\left|u\\right|+C$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x} - 2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nUse a connected real interval on which the integrand is defined.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{4 e^{2 x} - 4}{- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=2\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\ln\\left|- 2 x + e^{2 x} + 1\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186679466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3320dec6-8b4f-590e-8dcb-0742047d4bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(e^{2 x} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3320dec6-8b4f-590e-8dcb-0742047d4bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(e^{2 x} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679445} :answer-field/id #uuid "947510be-5291-5029-825b-05037b47652f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679486}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69d1eec5-81a3-5284-a785-7a97dc40fb64" :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 4 \\sqrt{e^{2 x} + 1} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}+C$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 1>0$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=e^{2 x} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 e^{2 x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{2 x} + 1>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 4 \\sqrt{e^{2 x} + 1} e^{2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=2\\left(\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=2\\left(\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n2\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(e^{2 x} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186679474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8156d017-3401-5bd3-a0d0-6313ab57906c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(2 \\sqrt{e^{x} + 3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8156d017-3401-5bd3-a0d0-6313ab57906c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left(2 \\sqrt{e^{x} + 3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679479} :answer-field/id #uuid "396dc548-1d55-579a-8b96-6c2d63e31740" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186679451}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79fd7a1c-f80b-5f30-b249-83e9987f33c8" :question/problem "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3 e^{x}}{\\sqrt{e^{x} + 3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nYou may use $\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=2 \\sqrt{u}+C$.\n\nWork where $e^{x} + 3>0$.\n\n{{answer}} $+C$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=e^{x} + 3$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(e^{x})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWork where $e^{x} + 3>0$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{3 e^{x}}{\\sqrt{e^{x} + 3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=3\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the supplied primitive:\n\n$$\n3\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=3\\left(2 \\sqrt{u}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n3\\left(2 \\sqrt{e^{x} + 3}\\right)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186679478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0112c27e-e043-5b29-ad36-2fcf89749094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\